00:15:28.193 --> 00:15:30.996
Ugh!

00:15:31.096 --> 00:15:33.565
I think the lad needs to
lose about fifteen pounds.

00:15:37.068 --> 00:15:40.472
I don't think it should
be that hard. All right,
there you go.

00:15:40.572 --> 00:15:42.774
TROY: Finally got to do one.
SAM: Yeah.

00:15:42.874 --> 00:15:48.614
The optimum outcome from this
whole diving exercise for me
would be a mess of sea

00:15:48.714 --> 00:15:52.150
cucumbers, 'cause I
haven't had them for
twenty-five years.

00:15:52.251 --> 00:15:54.653
And actually, I've been
craving them ever since.

00:15:54.753 --> 00:15:59.357
But without having a diver
living here, you're S-O-L.

00:15:59.457 --> 00:16:00.793
Ugh!

00:16:00.893 --> 00:16:02.628
We're having some fun.

00:16:07.899 --> 00:16:09.034
And he's away.

00:16:09.134 --> 00:16:11.302
He's off to the races.

00:16:11.402 --> 00:16:14.305
This is a first for me
and seeing is how I'm
not a diver

00:16:14.405 --> 00:16:15.974
and I don't have a
bunch of gear,

00:16:16.074 --> 00:16:21.146
if something does happen to
him when he's down there
he's, he's on his own.

00:16:30.655 --> 00:16:32.891
OLIVER: It's one AM.

00:16:32.991 --> 00:16:36.995
Parking on the grid here at
night because, uh, the
tides are gonna be kind of

00:16:37.095 --> 00:16:42.534
small tomorrow afternoon
and I'm not sure if I'll be
able to get on and off.

00:16:42.634 --> 00:16:45.503
Sometimes, the only time
that presents itself is right
in the middle of the night.

00:16:45.604 --> 00:16:49.274
It's not optimal, but
it's just what we got.

00:16:49.374 --> 00:16:52.110
This is the first time that
we've grid-ed this boat, so.

00:16:52.210 --> 00:16:55.214
Little nervous about it.

00:16:55.314 --> 00:16:56.815
A lot going on when
you grid this thing.

00:16:56.915 --> 00:17:00.685
You gotta make sure the
pilings are just right,
everything's lined up,

00:17:00.786 --> 00:17:04.156
don't want to smash the
rudder with the weight
of the own boat.

00:17:04.256 --> 00:17:07.359
I don't want to be
someone that someone
hears about one day.

00:17:09.227 --> 00:17:10.562
Little nervous.
Little nervous.

00:17:10.662 --> 00:17:13.432
I don't know where the
beach is over here.

00:17:17.636 --> 00:17:18.604
BREANNA: What's going on?

00:17:23.108 --> 00:17:25.043
OLIVER: There it
is, we're close.

00:17:25.143 --> 00:17:26.745
Slow down a little
bit here, whoa.

00:17:31.449 --> 00:17:33.785
Yeah, real close. Whoa.

00:17:33.885 --> 00:17:35.454
Pull the bow in.

00:17:41.326 --> 00:17:43.461
Oh, ouch.

00:17:43.562 --> 00:17:44.796
Holy moly.

00:17:45.830 --> 00:17:48.700
I gotta get out there. Find
something to tie it to.

00:17:51.703 --> 00:17:54.906
Don't really have
much to tie it to.

00:17:55.007 --> 00:17:56.074
All right.

00:17:56.174 --> 00:17:58.643
BREANNA: I'm a little
anxious about all this.

00:17:58.743 --> 00:18:03.848
It's definitely gonna
settle, uh, as the tide goes
out, so we just don't know

00:18:03.949 --> 00:18:06.151
how it's going to settle.
Bad things can happen

00:18:06.251 --> 00:18:12.224
where a boat will shift
and then it can roll
completely over so,

00:18:12.324 --> 00:18:14.660
we'll see. It's
probably not gonna be
a lot of sleep.

00:18:25.203 --> 00:18:28.239
OLIVER: Let's see
what we got, huh?

00:18:28.339 --> 00:18:32.443
Careful coming off this
ladder, Breanna, it's
real sketchy, okay?

00:18:32.543 --> 00:18:36.781
BREANNA: It's a new boat and
until you get the boat on the
grid and see what's going on,

00:18:36.881 --> 00:18:39.284
you really don't
know what you've got.

00:18:39.384 --> 00:18:42.354
You're hoping that the money
that you've already paid

00:18:42.454 --> 00:18:44.256
you don't have to put
any more into it.

00:18:46.958 --> 00:18:47.893
How's it look down there?

00:18:52.330 --> 00:18:53.632
How's it look?

00:18:53.732 --> 00:18:56.134
I'm wondering, curious.

00:18:56.234 --> 00:18:59.270
OLIVER: It's, it's, it's
(bleep) perfect dude,

00:18:59.370 --> 00:19:02.106
it's better than I could
have hoped for man.
BREANNA: Awesome.

00:19:02.207 --> 00:19:04.342
OLIVER: Yeah, I mean it's.
BREANNA: I'm gonna
come check it out.

00:19:04.442 --> 00:19:07.578
OLIVER: Hell, yeah.

00:19:07.679 --> 00:19:10.816
BREANNA: Wow, it
does look great.

00:19:10.916 --> 00:19:11.850
Sweet!

00:19:13.251 --> 00:19:15.620
Yeah, it looks like he
just freshly painted it.

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:19.724
OLIVER: Man, woo, yeah
see that's pretty much
what needs to be done,

00:19:19.824 --> 00:19:23.695
the zinc's gotta be replaced.
They're all ate away and

00:19:23.795 --> 00:19:26.999
that one there's kinda
gone and that bugger there.

00:19:27.099 --> 00:19:30.869
But, boy, and I think we
got what we paid for, man.

00:19:30.969 --> 00:19:34.306
We definitely got a
good deal here really.

00:19:34.406 --> 00:19:36.841
Well, let's get cracking man.
BREANNA: Sweet.

00:19:36.942 --> 00:19:39.845
OLIVER: Hoping I can
get this done before the
tide comes in around me.

00:19:49.754 --> 00:19:52.723
TROY: I probably have
almost a thousand dives
under my belt.

00:19:52.824 --> 00:19:58.630
But that doesn't mean
anything. It really is
a different world.

00:19:59.464 --> 00:20:03.969
You know, we're not meant
to be forty feet underwater.

00:20:04.069 --> 00:20:08.206
You can never get
overconfident about
diving at all.

00:20:08.306 --> 00:20:13.211
SAM: Twenty-five years ago, my
buddy spent, I'm going to say
fifteen minutes down there.

00:20:13.311 --> 00:20:17.182
Came up with a nice bag of
them hanging off his belt.

00:20:17.282 --> 00:20:20.151
And we had a hell
of a good feed.

00:20:20.251 --> 00:20:21.954
Fond memories.

00:20:24.823 --> 00:20:29.728
TROY: I get in the
water here, visibility
is not awful.

00:20:29.828 --> 00:20:33.599
But I see there's sand,
crushed shells everywhere.

00:20:39.804 --> 00:20:44.509
Looks like the otters had
a field day with, uh, just
about everything down there.

00:20:48.780 --> 00:20:52.583
SAM: He's been down
there, uh, oh, half
hour now anyways.

00:20:52.683 --> 00:20:56.888
I'm expecting that, uh,
seeing how he hasn't pulled
on this thing sooner

00:20:56.988 --> 00:20:59.091
probably pretty slim pickings.

00:21:19.311 --> 00:21:22.381
Oh, I hear the ladder
rattling. There he is.
TROY: Oh, God.

00:21:22.481 --> 00:21:24.950
SAM: There he is.

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:26.384
What'd you see?

00:21:26.484 --> 00:21:28.720
TROY: Definitely you
could tell the sea
otters have been here.

00:21:28.820 --> 00:21:32.157
A lot of eaten mussels and,
uh, yeah. Shells everywhere.

00:21:32.257 --> 00:21:36.794
They have, they have been
feasting here, no doubt.

00:21:36.895 --> 00:21:41.399
Not a single sign of sea
cucumber. You hear that?

00:21:41.499 --> 00:21:43.535
SAM: Yeah. I hear that.

00:21:43.635 --> 00:21:44.803
TROY: Oh, yeah.

00:21:44.903 --> 00:21:47.839
SAM: Yeah. Steady stream.

00:21:47.939 --> 00:21:50.508
It's coming out of this
one fitting right here.

00:21:50.608 --> 00:21:52.678
TROY: That explains the
air going pretty quick.

00:21:54.379 --> 00:21:59.550
Huh. It's not easy to
get this stuff out to the
dock shop or get it back.

00:21:59.650 --> 00:22:01.053
It is not quick.

00:22:01.153 --> 00:22:02.954
It's not cheap.

00:22:03.054 --> 00:22:06.291
SAM: It's a bunch of BS. BC.
TROY: Yeah. (inaudible)

00:22:08.894 --> 00:22:12.964
BREANNA: Yeah, it just
needs a little hose down.

00:22:13.065 --> 00:22:17.235
OLIVER: These are our
zincs right here that
we gotta replace.

00:22:17.335 --> 00:22:20.772
I'm hoping that everything
works out for them.

00:22:20.872 --> 00:22:23.574
Zincs are basically
grounded into the
steel here.

00:22:23.675 --> 00:22:26.344
It just is where the
electrical stuff that's
like where the ground of

00:22:26.444 --> 00:22:28.746
your batteries hooked to
is all into this stuff
it's all connected.

00:22:28.846 --> 00:22:32.951
And it just basically is a
sacrificial metal so that
stuff like the prop

00:22:33.051 --> 00:22:36.321
and the shaft and the rudder
doesn't get ate away.

00:22:44.862 --> 00:22:46.598
And this is the last zinc.

00:22:46.698 --> 00:22:49.634
Just in time as the
water is coming up.

00:22:55.606 --> 00:22:58.809
Oh. Doesn't fit.

00:22:58.910 --> 00:23:00.912
Hey, Breanna?
BREANNA: Yeah?

00:23:01.012 --> 00:23:02.313
OLIVER: You wanna
come on down here?

00:23:02.413 --> 00:23:04.749
BREANNA: Okay.

00:23:04.849 --> 00:23:06.484
OLIVER: So, we have kinda
something going on here,

00:23:06.584 --> 00:23:10.855
we gotta try and make
this bugger fit where
this one came off of.

00:23:10.955 --> 00:23:13.057
It's kind of
presenting a challenge.

00:23:13.158 --> 00:23:17.094
It could just come off and
then my whole shaft would have
no electrolysis protection.

00:23:17.194 --> 00:23:18.196
BREANNA: It'll get pitted.

00:23:18.296 --> 00:23:20.365
It'll start getting eaten
away by the electrolysis.

00:23:20.465 --> 00:23:21.799
OLIVER: Yeah. And
it's something,

00:23:21.900 --> 00:23:24.302
this is easily a
thousand dollar or more
propeller right here,

00:23:24.402 --> 00:23:25.870
and I can't afford
that right now.

00:23:25.970 --> 00:23:28.606
All my money right now has
to go into boat payments.

00:23:28.706 --> 00:23:33.444
With this tide coming in,
you think I have enough
time to grab the grinder?

00:23:33.545 --> 00:23:35.980
BREANNA: If you're
fast, I think we have
a little bit of time.

00:23:36.080 --> 00:23:37.248
I understand.
OLIVER: Right. And, well,

00:23:37.348 --> 00:23:39.684
if I can find it, and find
my cutting wheel and stuff.

00:23:39.784 --> 00:23:40.685
BREANNA: All right.

00:23:48.726 --> 00:23:51.729
OLIVER: Definitely the tides
starting to come back in,

00:23:51.829 --> 00:23:53.364
and if we don't get off
the beach on this tide,

00:23:53.465 --> 00:23:55.633
we're going to be stuck
there for quite a while.

00:23:55.733 --> 00:23:56.668
Not gonna be good.

00:24:05.109 --> 00:24:07.612
DAVID: Yeah, just be good
and ginger on the corners.

00:24:07.712 --> 00:24:09.714
Go one at a time instead of
trying to two hand them,

00:24:09.814 --> 00:24:11.682
if it'd be easier,
'cause like I say,

00:24:11.782 --> 00:24:14.085
we got no margin for
error when it comes to
material loss.

00:24:19.691 --> 00:24:24.929
I've done some
construction but most of
it is self-taught and, uh,

00:24:25.030 --> 00:24:28.766
the nature of the beast is
often necessity, it's just
how it is.

00:24:28.866 --> 00:24:30.735
We're getting
somewhere now, darling.

00:24:30.835 --> 00:24:33.438
Um, one, two, three,
four, five.

00:24:41.613 --> 00:24:44.015
Don't like that much.

00:24:44.115 --> 00:24:46.117
That's sitting
high in the middle.

00:24:46.217 --> 00:24:48.752
Can't have that going on.

00:24:48.853 --> 00:24:50.288
KRISTINA: Why is
that happening?

00:24:50.388 --> 00:24:53.057
DAVID: Uh, there might be,
uh, a little too much
gravel humped up on

00:24:53.157 --> 00:24:54.859
the middle there on one side.

00:24:54.959 --> 00:24:56.160
KRISTINA: That's not
gonna work on the chimney.

00:24:56.260 --> 00:25:00.598
DAVID: Right. Yeah.

00:25:00.699 --> 00:25:02.233
All right.

00:25:02.333 --> 00:25:05.103
This simply will not do.

00:25:05.203 --> 00:25:09.374
The, the whole, the whole base
plate is, is a little bit, uh,
convex and I need it to be

00:25:09.474 --> 00:25:12.744
concave so that the,
instead of spreading out
as I build the chimney up

00:25:12.844 --> 00:25:14.813
it pushes in on itself.

00:25:14.913 --> 00:25:18.849
I'm gonna have to figure
out what to do about you.

00:25:18.949 --> 00:25:19.985
Hm.

00:25:30.895 --> 00:25:32.397
DAVID: All right, baby.

00:25:32.497 --> 00:25:37.936
So, to make sure
the chimney doesn't start
trying to split apart on us.

00:25:42.507 --> 00:25:45.844
I'm gonna jack this up here,
I got some nice pressure
treated material there

00:25:45.944 --> 00:25:47.612
that's actually a wedge
KRISTINA: Okay.

00:25:47.712 --> 00:25:50.314
DAVID: And as soon as you
lift this up, I'm gonna try
to pound that into the, uh,

00:25:50.415 --> 00:25:53.951
the very edge of the, uh,
the framework on both sides
and that should be enough to,

00:25:54.051 --> 00:25:56.555
to get the concave
form off the base.

00:26:02.994 --> 00:26:07.432
Yeah. Definitely already
got some weight on her.

00:26:17.575 --> 00:26:20.412
Sweet, I don't
have to pound crap.

00:26:23.347 --> 00:26:27.185
Okay. Let her down.

00:26:31.923 --> 00:26:33.491
KRISTINA: There it is.

00:26:33.591 --> 00:26:38.730
DAVID: Oh, yeah. Look at
that. It's all this brick.

00:26:38.830 --> 00:26:41.566
Don't you love it when a plan
eventually comes together?

00:26:51.475 --> 00:26:53.378
OLIVER: The tide is coming
in kind of quick right now,

00:26:53.478 --> 00:26:54.813
and I'm rushing
against the clock.

00:27:00.585 --> 00:27:08.259
All righty. Grab one of
these for extra just in
case, always be prepared.

00:27:08.360 --> 00:27:11.262
All right. Let's
get out of here.

00:27:15.767 --> 00:27:17.068
Since it was our first
time griding the boat,

00:27:17.168 --> 00:27:19.770
we didn't really know
what zincs to buy.

00:27:19.871 --> 00:27:25.043
So, I kinda just guessed
and got what I figured
you know, would work.

00:27:25.143 --> 00:27:30.481
I'm just going to buzz off a
little bit of this end here.

00:27:31.282 --> 00:27:33.084
One of the props didn't
really fit right,

00:27:33.184 --> 00:27:35.186
so I'm definitely
concerned about that one.

00:27:35.286 --> 00:27:38.656
The prop is an important part
of the boat, you know, if
that starts to get, you know,

00:27:38.756 --> 00:27:41.392
eaten away with
electrolysis, it's going
to be an expensive fix.

00:27:41.492 --> 00:27:44.261
And so I'm hoping
everything works out.

00:27:44.361 --> 00:27:46.798
All right. Breanna, just
hang on to this bugger
like that, okay?

00:27:46.898 --> 00:27:49.367
I'm going to cut off on
this thing down here.

00:27:56.975 --> 00:27:57.975
All right.

00:27:58.710 --> 00:28:01.279
All right, it's rough
enough to go on my boat.

00:28:01.379 --> 00:28:03.715
Even it was too nice it
wouldn't know what to do.

00:28:11.856 --> 00:28:13.758
Okay. With the tide coming
in around my ankles,

00:28:13.858 --> 00:28:16.828
I just have to roll around
with what I got right now.

00:28:19.097 --> 00:28:20.898
BREANNA: Oh.

00:28:20.998 --> 00:28:23.301
OLIVER: Cool. Yeah,
sweet man.

00:28:23.401 --> 00:28:25.903
Well, definitely got it on,
and definitely is tight.

00:28:26.003 --> 00:28:29.707
So far, I'm a happy camper.
BREANNA: At the nick of time.
OLIVER: Heck yeah.

00:28:29.807 --> 00:28:32.510
Only thing left to do is
get off this thing and go
catch a fish.

00:28:32.610 --> 00:28:34.446
BREANNA: Let's go
see what it's got.

00:28:39.684 --> 00:28:40.618
OLIVER: Come on, dogs.
Load up!

00:28:44.188 --> 00:28:47.425
All aboard?
BREANNA: Yup.

00:28:47.525 --> 00:28:49.694
OLIVER: Moment of truth.

00:28:51.896 --> 00:28:54.499
Oh, we're moving.
Hell yeah.

00:28:57.135 --> 00:28:58.602
All right.

00:28:58.702 --> 00:29:02.307
There's sun shines on a dog's
ass every now and again.

00:29:04.575 --> 00:29:06.510
SAM: Oh, look at that.
TROY: Oh, yeah.

00:29:06.610 --> 00:29:10.047
SAM: Well, you cannot hear
it but you can sure as
hell see it.

00:29:10.147 --> 00:29:11.915
I think you got a
faulty hose here.

00:29:12.016 --> 00:29:13.884
But we can put a
band aid on it.

00:29:13.984 --> 00:29:18.589
This is a self-fusing tape;
it fuses itself together.

00:29:18.689 --> 00:29:22.927
I patched a garden
hose with it once.

00:29:23.027 --> 00:29:24.496
TROY: Let's try it on
the life support system.

00:29:26.698 --> 00:29:29.066
It's gonna be a thing
where I'm gonna have to
keep a much, much better eye

00:29:29.167 --> 00:29:32.603
on the pressure gauge.
I mean it doesn't
matter if you've dove

00:29:32.703 --> 00:29:34.973
hundreds of thousands of
times your next dive's your
most important dive,

00:29:35.073 --> 00:29:39.944
I take very, very,
very seriously.

00:29:40.044 --> 00:29:41.913
SAM: I wanna give this
a good soaping down now.

00:29:44.615 --> 00:29:46.483
TROY: Now that
looks, looks good.

00:29:46.584 --> 00:29:48.086
SAM: Well.

00:29:48.186 --> 00:29:50.755
TROY: Horseshoes
and hand grenades.

00:29:50.855 --> 00:29:53.691
CARL: Okay, let's
try that again.
MORGAN: Okay.

00:29:53.791 --> 00:29:56.127
CARL: Oh lordy, lordy,
lordy, look who's forty.

00:30:01.065 --> 00:30:03.168
Okay, I'm gonna go for it.
MORGAN: Give her hell, baby.

00:30:10.775 --> 00:30:12.643
CARL: Oh, I hope this works.

00:30:12.743 --> 00:30:15.680
Keep this whole
thing from sinking.

00:30:18.583 --> 00:30:20.185
SAM: We should be good right
here, just stop right here.

00:30:23.454 --> 00:30:28.860
TROY: Time to start
gearing up. And then
let's get wet.

00:30:50.447 --> 00:30:54.352
Although Sammy science
works ninety-nine-point
nine percent of the time,

00:30:54.452 --> 00:30:57.321
this is my life we're dealing
with here with this air, so,

00:30:57.421 --> 00:30:59.624
we don't want to be going
down super deep with, uh,

00:30:59.724 --> 00:31:03.361
with a hose that's not
quite at a hundred percent.

00:31:07.731 --> 00:31:12.603
SAM: He's still breathing.
I'm not a diver.

00:31:12.703 --> 00:31:16.440
I catch things with a hook
and line, maybe a gill net.

00:31:16.540 --> 00:31:21.946
The idea of going in there
and trying to get something
with my bare hands.(laughs)

00:31:22.046 --> 00:31:24.582
I have a philosophy.
Stay out of the water.

00:31:30.321 --> 00:31:32.323
Been in the water a
long time.

00:31:32.423 --> 00:31:35.459
Gotta be just about
out of air now.

00:31:35.559 --> 00:31:37.595
I know.

00:31:37.695 --> 00:31:40.231
Papa's getting
farther from the boat.

00:31:45.736 --> 00:31:48.005
SAM: There are some
little pocket caves down
here along this cliff.

00:31:48.105 --> 00:31:50.541
It is aways down and
hopefully he can make it
to those.

00:31:51.909 --> 00:31:56.080
There are little pockets
of life I'm hoping the sea
otter can't deal with

00:31:56.180 --> 00:31:57.749
because it's on the
wrong spot.

00:32:01.119 --> 00:32:02.287
He's coming back.

00:32:12.630 --> 00:32:16.934
I thought he might be
coming up with the basket,
by God he is.

00:32:17.034 --> 00:32:17.802
TROY: Whoa-ho!

00:32:21.038 --> 00:32:24.242
SAM: What did you get?
TROY: We got a little one.

00:32:24.342 --> 00:32:27.378
SAM: Well, oh I see him.

00:32:27.478 --> 00:32:32.283
That is what you call pretty
thin soup, pretty thin soup.

00:32:32.383 --> 00:32:34.618
TROY: That's not going
to fill the freezer.

00:32:34.718 --> 00:32:37.522
SAM: No, it's not.

